  "strategy": "1. Approach and rationale\nThe central idea is to use a genetic algorithm with differential evolution to explore the solution space more thoroughly than local optimization methods. This addresses the bottleneck of local optima by allowing global search through population-based mutation and crossover. The algorithm evolves populations of candidate solutions that satisfy the integral constraint and aim to minimize the maximum overlap integral C\u2085. This approach balances exploration and exploitation while maintaining feasibility through continuous constraint satisfaction.\n\n2. Mathematical specification\nLet h \u2208 [0,1]^n_points be the step function vector with dx = 2/n_points. The objective is to minimize:\nC\u2085 = max_k \u222bh(x)(1 - h(x + k)) dx\nThis evaluates to max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\") * dx) over all lags k.\n\nConstraints:\n- 0 \u2264 h[i] \u2264 1 for all i\n- \u2211h[i] = n_points / 2 (equivalent to \u222bh dx = 1)\n\nThe genetic algorithm maintains a population of h vectors, each satisfying the constraints. Fitness is calculated as the maximum overlap integral. Mutation introduces diversity, while crossover combines features of high-fitness individuals to generate new candidates. The population is pruned to retain only the best performing individuals.\n\n3. Implementation procedure\na. Parent validation: If initial_h_values is provided, use it as the initial population. Otherwise, generate a random initial population by:\n   i. Creating n_population vectors with randomly selected positions for h[i] = 1\n   ii. Normalizing each vector to ensure \u2211h == n_points / 2\n\nb. Initialization:\n   - n_population = 30\n   - n_points = 400\n   - dx = 2.0 / n_points\n   - For each vector in the population:\n      - Randomly select n_points / 2 indices to set h[i] = 1\n      - Normalize the vector by scaling to ensure \u2211h == n_points / 2\n\nc. Optimization loop:\n   i. For each generation (up to 600 seconds):\n      1. Evaluate fitness for all population members: compute max(np.correlate(h, 1-h, mode=\"full\") * dx)\n      2. Select top 20% of individuals based on fitness\n      3. Generate new candidates through differential evolution:\n         - For each new candidate, select three random individuals from the top 20%\n         - Compute a mutant vector using: h_mutant = h_a + F*(h_b - h_c)\n         - Clip values to [0,1]\n         - Normalize the mutated vector to ensure \u2211h == n_points / 2\n      4. Replace the worst 50% of the population with the new candidates\n      5. Track the best solution found so far\n\nd. Termination:\n   - Stop after reaching the time budget or when the best solution improves for 50 consecutive generations\n   - Return the best solution and its C\u2085 value\n\n4. Search choices and useful variations\na. Default configuration:\n   - n_population = 30\n   - n_points = 400\n   - Mutation factor F = 0.5\n   - Crossover probability = 1.0\n   - Number of generations = 2000\n   - Fitness evaluation: use the provided evaluator's method for exact score calculation\n\nb. Variations:\n   1. Population size tuning: 20-50 individuals\n   2. Mutation factor F: 0.4 to 0.8\n   3. Different crossover strategies\n   4. Adaptive mutation rates based on convergence\n\n5. Compute budget and robustness\na. Time allocation:\n   - Differential evolution steps dominate the runtime (90%)\n   - Fitness evaluation for 30 vectors per generation\n   - Early termination after 50 consecutive generations with no improvement\n\nb. Robustness measures:\n   - Fallback to initial solution if population optimization fails\n   - Gradient checks for numerical stability in mutation steps\n   - Population pruning ensures feasibility through normalization\n   - Adaptive mutation rates prevent premature convergence\n\nc. Numerical stability:\n   - Clip values to [0,1] after mutation\n   - Normalization after every mutation/crossover step\n   - Avoid division by zero by using a small epsilon in normalization\n\n6. Validation and return contract\na. Feasibility checks:\n   - Verify all h[i] \u2208 [0,1] after every mutation/crossover\n   - Confirm \u2211h == n_points / 2 after normalization\n   - Ensure the correlation computation matches the evaluator's method\n\nb. Objective validation:\n   - Re-evaluate the best solution using the provided evaluator\n   - Compare with the previous best value to ensure improvement\n\nc. Interface requirements:\n   - Return h_values as a list of floats\n   - Provide c5_bound as the computed maximum overlap\n   - Include n_points as the number of samples\n\nd. Sanity checks:\n   - Check that the correlation calculation includes all lags\n   - Verify that the normalization step maintains the integral constraint\n   - Ensure that the returned h_values array has length n_points",
